Z||000000||sqoach||12-19-2000||12:56 AM||||ok, as a lot of you know, i've had problems with my car overheating. i bought a new radiator cap (16 psi, is that too high?) i think the stock one was 13 psi. i took it for a drive tonight to see how well it would do. it did just fine. drove for 45 minutes in town. the weird thing is, that my temp gauge never went past the 2nd mark. i'm guessing that's about 140 temp?? anyway, i know this isn't normal. but it did seem to react normal. when i sit and idle for a bit, it goes up some, then when i start driving again, it goes back down.<P>i do have a new 195 stat, but i took the o-ring off of it cuz when i took it out one time, it stretched out and wouldn't go back in right. the stat still fit in there pretty good. i know some coolant can get by, but don't think enough to keep it running that cool. it's probably a temp sensor or temp gauge problem but i'm not sure which. any thoughts?? also, do you think that new cap could have fixed my overheating? thanks.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||2xsess||12-19-2000||01:17 AM||||Yes if your cap wasn't holding pressure then a new one would fix your problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||12-19-2000||12:55 PM||||well if it did fix it, that's good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>but what about the temp???||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||12-19-2000||01:07 PM||||hmmm. I seem to remembet mention the radiator cap when you first mentioned the problem *grin*. Maybe I didn't.<P>For the temp I would make sure all the air is out of the system first.||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||12-19-2000||02:01 PM||||yeah i think you did  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>i just decided to go buy one since they don't cost much. it's the locking kind. don't know if they help any more than the other.<P>anyways, i've burped it about 2 weeks ago when i got the new cap and the thermostat. i raise up the back end, take off radiator cap, fill up from the thermostat housing, put cap back on front when it pours out, top off in back, then close it up. is that the right method? or should i let it run for a minute after topping it off then fill again???||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-19-2000||07:30 PM||||That's about right for a top off. I usually run it awhile then let it cool and check it again just to make sure.<P>Even if the 13# cap works perfectly it can still cause overheating. (From a coming cave article)<P>When you spin a water pump you create areas of low pressure on the back of the impeller vanes. This causes air disolved in the water to explode into bubbles. This is called cavitation. A pump that is cavitating looses large chunks of efficiancy.<P>Increasing the water pressure reduces cavitation and thus makes the pump more effective and it moves much more water thru the engine.<P>A 2-3 PSI diference can have a big impact on how efficient the system is. Fiero's very long plumbing needs all the pumping power available.||||reg||1
Z||000006||HAGS||12-20-2000||05:17 AM||||My engine overheats alot too, I've changed the oil and regularly change/add coolant but when I have to stop it quickly rises to 220-260, I have to throw it in park and it usually goes back down to 220, then I rev it at varrying RPMs and it drops back down to 160 then slowly comes back up....then green light and it sputters for about 10 feet then either starts to accelerate or dies......if it accelerates then I race up to speed and it drops to 110-120.  It usually doesnt get up to 260 very often but today it did, I pulled over and added coolant and it stayed at 120 the rest of the day, even at lights or stop signs....  can someone post the procedure to changing/adding coolant?  All I do is take off the resevoir cap, fill it up, take off the radiator cap, fill that resevoir up, then take off.  I see no visible leaks when I spend time under my car.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||12-20-2000||06:53 AM||||The Stant radiator cap that AutoZone sells for the Fiero is the wrong one.  It should be a non-vented cap.  They list a vented cap.  With our radiators being lower than the engine the water will trickle out of the radiator when the system is shut down and cools.<P>There was a thread about this some time back.<P>Technically, cavitation has nothing to do with air being in the system.  Cavitation is actually boiling of the liquid being pumped due to the low pressure on the front side of the impeller.  Pressure is reduced to the point where the liquid flashes to vapor.  This happens rather violently  and the colapsing of the vapor bubbles is damaging to the impeller.  Air in the system causes an impeller to be airlocked which reduces system efficiency.<P>Take care and have a Great Christmas and New Year,<P>Carroll<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 12-20-2000).]||||reg||1
